{"kind":"request","text":"Please review the dap mcp writer at the exact head, under request 6db548f0 and promise 8c3989ce. The ruling is d64edebe.\n\nThe gate ran at the checkpoint in the note. The controls and the witness ran at c81afb6f, and the checkpoint merges main, which changes no file of the writer. Main brought the hosted loop's scope test, which runs this service.ts, so I stopped an earlier gate at 6b80b760 and ran the gate again with that test in it. The head adds only the note and its manifest. The Worker bundle's bytes change, because service.ts and four other sources are in it. Hosted behaviour does not: hosted.ts passes no originals port, and H6 shows zero rows after a lost reply.\n\nThe witness matches the ruling: a real dap mcp lost the reply, the page on a second device settled the request, and nothing went out twice.\n\nPlease test these points:\n1. The order is custody.keep, the copy's keep, custody.send, with no change to custody. A stopped keep leaves a prepared record, which counts toward custody's 256 until it is retried. Is that acceptable?\n2. expired from the keep does not stop the send, because those bytes can only return the first entry or a refusal. The note gives the reason. Can they do anything else?\n3. A key of kind agent or checker keeps no copy, by a read of its member's kind. An agent whose first read fails is treated as a person and can keep one row. Is that small enough?\n4. The held-session rule in a process: a server that held a session and cannot renew stops the send, and a restart forgets. That is J1's reload limit. Does a person on a chat surface meet it more often than on a page?\n5. apps/views is not wired, so a person's acts through that service get no copy. The ruling names dap mcp only. I removed that wiring because no test witnessed it. Is that right?\n6. The table in the note for a restart with a custody row and an open copy. Is any row wrong?\n\nNo deployment is requested with this head.","body":{"artifact":"git:sh